diff --git a/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md b/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..aad6ca5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/figma/inventory-assets.md
@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+# Inventory panel: baked images
+
+These are the visuals from the inventory panel design (`design/figma/inventory-panel.json`, screen
+`12:340`, Slot set `20:1`) that become uploaded images. Everything else is built from UI objects:
+fills, gradients, rounded corners, strokes, the panel and text shadows, the selected-slot glow, and
+all text.
+
+All asset ids, sheet sizes, and sprite rects live in
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/UI/InventoryAssets.luau`. Every id there is a placeholder
+(`rbxassetid://0`) for now. While an id is a placeholder, the UI draws a stand-in: a rounded square
+in the block's color for block art, an "X" for the close icon, no lock icon (the "Lv N" label still
+shows), and no hex pattern.
+
+## Export size
+
+The UI is authored for a 1080-tall screen and grows up to 2x on a 4K screen (`Screen.MaxScale`), so
+**everything is exported at 2x** its size in the design. Every side stays under 1024 pixels.
+
+Every image needs **alpha bleed**: carry the edge color out into the transparent pixels (the "dilate"
+or "solidify" step in image tools). Roblox turns transparent pixels black on upload, and without
+bleed, soft edges get a dark fringe when scaled.
+
+## 1. Block art sheet
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| What | The 10 block pictures (the `Block Art` image fills, e.g. node `40:0` for Brick), each **with its drop shadow baked in** (black, 40% opacity, y offset 4, blur 8). Roblox's `UIShadow` follows a frame's rounded box, not an image's shape, so the shadow has to be in the image. |
+| Why an image | It's artwork, and the shadow follows the art's shape. |
+| Export | For each block, put the 56 x 56 art in a transparent **88 x 88** frame with the art at (16, 12), so the shadow fits inside with margin. Export each frame at 2x: **176 x 176**. |
+| Sheet | **880 x 352**, 5 columns x 2 rows of 176 px cells, in the order below. All ten show together whenever the panel opens, so they share one sheet: one download, and they load at the same time. |
+| In Roblox | `ImageLabel`, 88 x 88 at (4, 4) in the 96 x 96 slot, `ScaleType = Fit`, `ImageRectOffset` / `ImageRectSize` from `InventoryAssets.blockArt(name)`. Locked slots use the same art at `ImageTransparency = 0.6` (the design's 40% opacity). |
+
+| Cell | Block | Rect offset (px) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Row 1, column 1 | Brick | 0, 0 |
+| Row 1, column 2 | Dirt | 176, 0 |
+| Row 1, column 3 | Grass | 352, 0 |
+| Row 1, column 4 | Window | 528, 0 |
+| Row 1, column 5 | Wood | 704, 0 |
+| Row 2, column 1 | Stone | 0, 176 |
+| Row 2, column 2 | Sand | 176, 176 |
+| Row 2, column 3 | Ice | 352, 176 |
+| Row 2, column 4 | Lamp | 528, 176 |
+| Row 2, column 5 | Gold | 704, 176 |
+
+Every rect is 176 x 176. If you reorder or add blocks, update `BLOCK_ART_ORDER` in
+`InventoryAssets.luau` to match. Adding an 11th block means a new sheet: grow it to 3 rows (880 x 528)
+and upload it again.
+
+## 2. Icon sheet
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| What | `Icon/Close` (node `40:80`, 20 x 20) and `Icon/Lock` (node `40:100`, 24 x 24). |
+| Why an image | They're glyphs. Roblox has no object that draws vector shapes like these. |
+| Export | **White on transparent**, so Roblox can tint them with `ImageColor3`. Each one is centered in a 64 x 64 cell at 2x: close at 40 x 40, lock at 48 x 48. A cell stands for a 32 x 32 box in the UI. |
+| Sheet | **128 x 64**: close at offset (0, 0), lock at offset (64, 0), each 64 x 64. |
+| In Roblox | `ImageLabel` 32 x 32, centered (close: in the 44 x 44 round button; lock: 10 above the slot's center), `ScaleType = Fit`, `ImageColor3` = Text/Primary (245, 246, 250). |
+
+## 3. Hex pattern tile
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| What | The `Hex Pattern` group (node `40:78`): rows of 112 x 128 hexagons with 2 px white outlines, drawn behind the panel at 6% opacity. |
+| Why an image | It's 35 vector shapes of decoration that never changes. One tiled image costs one small texture; rebuilding it with UI objects would cost dozens. |
+| Export | Hide the group's 6% opacity and export the outlines at **full white**; Roblox sets the strength. Crop **112 x 192**, one full repeat of the pattern (two staggered rows), starting at frame position (602, 352), which is the panel's corner moved in past its 2 px border. Any 112 x 192 crop of the pattern tiles without a seam; this one also keeps the design's alignment. Export at 2x: **224 x 384**. |
+| In Roblox | `ImageLabel` inset 2 px inside the panel, with its own `UICorner` of 14 (the panel's 16 minus the inset), `ScaleType = Tile`, `TileSize = 112 x 192` offsets, `ImageColor3` white, `ImageTransparency = 0.94`. |
+
+## Uploading
+
+1. Upload the three PNGs (`InventoryBlockArt.png` 880 x 352, `InventoryIcons.png` 128 x 64,
+   `InventoryHexTile.png` 224 x 384) under the account or group that owns the experience, so the
+   game is allowed to load them.
+2. Put the ids in `InventoryAssets.luau`: `BLOCK_ART_IMAGE`, `ICON_IMAGE`, `HEX_PATTERN_IMAGE`.
+3. Check them in game: each image loads, looks sharp on a 4K screen, and each rect lands on the
+   right block or icon. On a phone too: some phones have been reported to show the wrong part of a
+   sprite sheet.
+4. Keep the PNGs and the Figma export frames next to `design/figma/` so a later re-export starts
+   from the same files.
